WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.320
I'm Regina Crimp.

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:02.320
Traci Reardon.

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:03.320
I'm Ho Ho.

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:04.320
I'm a naughty elf.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.330
My name is Big Sue I uh firmly believe in
everybody's right to have a slice of pizza

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:09.330
pie.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:10.330
My name is Todd.

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:12.269
I don't really care if I'm grounded.

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:21.109
My name is Lauren Lapkus and I'm an improvisor
and actor.

00:00:21.109 --> 00:00:25.580
So you might know Lauren from her roles in
Orange Is the New Black or Jurassic World.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:27.980
You might even know her from her own podcast.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:33.079
I however was introduced to her through the
podcast Comedy Bang Bang where she often improvises

00:00:33.079 --> 00:00:36.260
slightly unhinged oddball characters.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:41.690
On the 377th episode of that podcast, she
and Paul F. Tompkins created one of the most

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:44.920
delightful and weird comedic moments of the
last year.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:47.379
And it was all completely improvised.

00:00:47.379 --> 00:00:48.379
Chazmin: "We used to sleep in a vault."

00:00:48.379 --> 00:00:49.379
Scott Aukerman: "Really?"

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:50.800
Sunny: "Yeah that got tight.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.120
Once we started making money!"

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:54.670
Chazmin: "I think that's where we developed
a clausterphobia as well."

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:55.670
Sunny: "Maybe a little!"

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:59.679
Paul F. Tompkins and I played characters named
Chazmin and Sunny and all we decided before

00:00:59.679 --> 00:01:04.171
we recorded the episode was that were going
to be radio show hosts together and that we

00:01:04.171 --> 00:01:05.280
had this morning talk show.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:06.280
Chazmin: "I don't know how you feel about
it Sunny!"

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:07.280
Sunny: "The same you know that!"

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:08.729
Chazmin: "Ahhhh that was a trick question."

00:01:08.729 --> 00:01:13.100
Sunny: "Razzle dazzle!"

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:16.600
I think characters really spoke to me because
I could be anyone and especially because I

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:21.750
started when I was younger it felt more comfortable
to not play myself and to play something else

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.130
and something totally different.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:27.360
I find voices in my car all the time and one
of the most recent ones I found was Big Sue

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.619
who's a she talks like this.

00:01:29.619 --> 00:01:30.689
She likes pizza.

00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:31.689
Her big thing is pizza.

00:01:31.689 --> 00:01:34.119
She's always concerned about getting another
slice.

00:01:34.119 --> 00:01:37.270
Uh she describes pizza very in depth even
though we all know what that is.

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:41.680
Uh but finding that voice was really fun for
me cause it's like a new thing that I discovered

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:42.770
I could do.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:47.090
I definitely feel like I embody characters
as I'm doing a podcast.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:51.649
A lot of my characters have really absurd
descriptions.

00:01:51.649 --> 00:01:56.539
There's one character, I believe if I recall
correctly her eyes were extremely close together

00:01:56.539 --> 00:01:58.869
and her butt crack was fused.

00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:02.880
I have Pamela from Big Bear who's a meth head.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.070
She's got crazy teeth.

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.470
For sure missing some of them.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:10.730
And then there's Ho Ho the Naughty Elf who's
the size of a dollar bill uh he's a naughty

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:13.350
elf that works in Santa's workshop.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:15.500
He has a candy cane penis?

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.750
Can I say that?

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:19.410
He has a candy cane penis.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:22.000
He's evil but lovable.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.440
One of the things that gets hard the more
episodes of my podcast I do is finding new

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:27.440
voices.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:28.440
It's basically impossible.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:31.070
I've done like 70 episodes now and it's a
different character every time.

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:36.000
So I have Traci Reardon who's a 17 year old
girl she talks like she has a rhotacism which

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.920
means she can't say her "r's".

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:43.050
Regina Crimp who she's the singer of The America's
Funniest Home Videos theme song.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:46.360
Her hair gets bigger it doesn't grow longer.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:51.810
She sings "We've got lives from coast to coast"
and she talks like this she really is like

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:56.540
she's just happy about life and you know she
lives in her car, sure, she lives in her car.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:03:01.310
She uh grills hamburgers on the engine some
nights just to kinda heat em up.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:03.350
She doesn't have a microwave or anything like
that.

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:04.350
She's pretty sad.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:09.060
She spent all of her money from her theme
songs uh pretty quickly and now she doesn't

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:10.060
have anything.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:12.210
When I was in Chicago there was a group called
Ragdolls.

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:14.320
An all female improv group.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:19.030
They were so amazing and to watch a group
of women just go totally insane together and

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:22.040
follow whatever was saying was completely
and totally support each other was really

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:23.710
inspiring to me.

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:28.380
As I was younger it was a lot harder for me
to start a conversation with a stranger for

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:34.540
instance and I think improv taught me how
to just go with it and now judge myself and

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:37.820
trust that I have something worth saying.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:40.040
Thanks so much for watching the video.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:45.180
I'm going to put a couple of links down below
with podcast recommendations for all of Lauren's

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:46.180
characters.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:50.760
They are all super funny they all had me in
tears by the end I was laughing so hard and

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:54.400
it's really some of the best comedy and improv
you'll ever listen to.

